WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.460
I think off of the pure fact that this episode
is primarily a flashback will be people’s

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.670
least favorite episode of the season.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.849
To me, it started out as my least favorite
after finishing it and the more I think about

00:00:10.849 --> 00:00:13.089
it, the more the episode grows on me.

00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:14.089
Can it be better?

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:18.369
Yes, and I’ll go over 2 main aspects in
a second that bothered me and I think could

00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:21.850
have increased the quality of the episode
if they were fixed.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.930
One of those main complaints is not the notion
of any of this being filler.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:30.679
Because we are doing a major exploration of
one of our main characters that further fleshes

00:00:30.679 --> 00:00:35.270
them out and brings even more context to the
sole being of Ellie’s character.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.490
We see the environment she grew up in which
explains her more tough persona, we see the

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:44.700
authoritative figures that hold power over
her life that fuels her rebellious nature,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.220
and we see her most prized and close relationship
fall to ruins which explains why she’s so

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.690
afraid of losing Joel.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:54.449
If you think about it, the writing in this
episode is brilliant.

00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:59.989
Neil Druckman did an outstanding job adapting
the DLC from the first game into this episode.

00:00:59.989 --> 00:01:04.610
Despite there being no current plot progression,
the episode is still very efficient on a scene-to-scene

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.760
basis as to developing Ellie more.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.600
That’s why I don’t understand why people
are calling this episode filler.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.250
It feels like they are just looking at it
from the perspective of solely plot progression

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:19.100
as if these characters are only an icon on
a board game moving from point A to B. If

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.329
anything this episode is the opposite of filler
because it focuses on a main character.

00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:28.940
To prove this, you could technically sandwich
these scenes in the middle of episode 1 when

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.180
we do the 20-year time jump.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.060
Because that would be where it mostly lines
up in the timeline.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.670
From there it would act as a great character
introduction for Ellie and both Joel and Ellie’s

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.630
past would be fleshed out at that point.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:44.890
Now I’m not arguing this should be the case
and I think this flashback episode is fine

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:49.450
where it is, but it adds to the story because
it deals with one of our main characters.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.880
With this same logic and if you think Ellie’s
flashback is filler, then by definition, that

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.500
would make Joel’s starting point filler
too.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.631
You may be thinking that I’m reaching here,
but in terms of structure for season 1, if

00:02:00.631 --> 00:02:05.090
you had Joel’s flashback in this episode
as an example, then it also wouldn’t progress

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.390
the current plot.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:10.780
However, Joel’s starting point is important
for the show because of how much character

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.270
progression and context we learn about it
him.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.420
And that’s why I think Ellie’s flashback
is almost as equally important.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.950
Throughout the show, we can pick up on details
about what happened to Ellie and why she’s

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:25.489
the way she is and that might be your reasoning
as to why we don’t need this flashback,

00:02:25.489 --> 00:02:27.459
but the same can be said about Joel.

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:31.150
Like with Ellie slowly learning that Joel
had a daughter before the outbreak.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.260
So overall, my point is that Ellie’s flashback
may seem more like filler because of where

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:41.459
it lies within the story and in reality, it’s
not because of how much it adds to her character.

00:02:41.459 --> 00:02:45.239
Sorry for that tangent, I feel like I needed
to fully explain that from how many people

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:47.319
I’ve seen complaining about this.

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:52.190
So moving away from the filler hot topic,
let’s go into the episode's strengths.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:56.190
By far the best aspect to me is the duo of
Ellie and Riley.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.349
They were riveting in basically every scene
they had together and her and Ellie exploring

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:03.489
the 5 wonders of the mall was surprisingly
wholesome.

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:07.170
Like I didn’t think an escalator could make
anyone this happy so the fact that Ellie is

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.349
this blown away is hilarious.

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:14.090
The episode overall harnesses the fish out
of water aspect really well and every area

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.210
of the mall they spend time in feels perfectly
utilized.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:21.650
Especially the stores because it brings up
the practicality of living in an apocalypse

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.099
and we can see the different outlandish things
we used in our society that are no longer

00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:27.790
relevant in theirs.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.590
Like soap obviously, who the hell uses that.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:35.489
But going back to Ellie and Riley, the acting
for both of them was outstanding in this episode.

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:40.189
I loved all the subtle details having to do
with Ellie being attracted to Riley.

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:47.209
Some examples include having Riley turn around
when Ellie was changing clothes, Ellie asking

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:51.220
Riley if the reason she joined the fireflies
was because of a boyfriend, but said under

00:03:51.220 --> 00:04:01.200
the guise of a joke to mask her intentions,
Ellie being giddy that Riley did all of this

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.180
for her, Ellie fixing her hair in the mirror,
and then a lot of other minor moments of Ellie

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.569
gazing at Riley with so much joy.

00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:13.220
Picking up on all of this is so much fun and
to me is the definition of great writing and

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:14.220
acting.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.250
The marriage between these two aspects of
the show and how well they synergize together

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.680
makes me sad that this episode is getting
downplayed so much.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:25.170
Because it gets even better from here when
they get bit by the infected and they have

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:29.220
to confront the crushing reality that the
love they just discovered between each other

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.220
is over.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.730
That’s extremely heartbreaking and again
the actors here nail the sorrow and rage that

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.130
comes along with this terrible reality.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:42.310
Bella Ramsey is flat-out amazing and she is
giving a performance of a lifetime at such

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:43.850
an early age.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:48.190
It makes me excited to see her in the future
as she grows even further as an actor.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:51.850
Although she’s already at a point where
she’s better than most actors who are even

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:53.190
older than her.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:56.310
Going into another aspect that I loved was
the production design.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.650
It’s been spectacular throughout the show,
but seeing a run-down mall with so much detail

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.490
in every square inch was astounding to see.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:08.090
Every surface withering away, the flickering
lights, the vegetation even making its way

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:12.210
into this space, the amount of shit on the
ground we can hear them walk on, and then

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.880
this being contrasted with more pristine areas
that people don’t care about like the arcade,

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:19.210
makes their inclusion even more exciting.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:23.500
It adds to this notion of them stepping into
this world before the outbreak and they get

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:28.250
to live a slice of life detached from all
the dogshit they have to deal with every day.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:34.430
It’s beautiful, happy, loud, bright, fun,
and everything about this space is an utter

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:39.030
contradiction about any other aspect previously
set up in this world.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:42.789
Making it that much more important to these
characters and it even feels surreal.

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:46.919
I think experiencing this moment at this point
in the story makes it so satisfying from how

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:50.819
treacherous and devoid of happiness Joel and
Ellie’s journey has been.

00:05:50.819 --> 00:05:54.780
These small happy moments are the reminder
of how great life can be and seeing these

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:59.540
characters live through them is special because
of how much they have to overcome overall.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:04.000
The 5 wonders of the world sequence has an
infectious aura of happiness tied to it and

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.030
I absolutely loved it.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.890
Even adding in the song Take on Me was a great
touch too.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:13.150
A couple of other minor things I loved about
this episode were the ways in which Ellie

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.740
bonding with Riley were shot and how they
set up the rogue infected in the mall.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:22.050
Most of the cinematography involving Ellie
being visually in love with Riley were generally

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.780
lit with warm colors.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:28.730
It gives off this feeling of safety and happiness
and it splashes onto the screen with so much

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:29.819
vibrance.

00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:33.010
Especially in comparison to the rest of what
the show looks like.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:37.039
Like the arcade scene being washed out in
pink in particular I feel like was visual

00:06:37.039 --> 00:06:39.380
foreshadowing of their romance.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:43.340
Then I think it was genius setting up the
single infected in the mall relatively early

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.919
because it creates a lot of suspense.

00:06:45.919 --> 00:06:50.630
It’s kind of like the Hitchcock method of
showing a bomb under a table and then continuing

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.640
on with the dialogue scene.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.210
Because once they show that infected, you
know he’s going to come back and you are

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:00.129
left with this feeling of oncoming doom that
can’t be avoided.

00:07:00.129 --> 00:07:04.930
So again, it’s sad to see this episode getting
hated on from how much fun it ended up being,

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:09.340
unless if people are also mad about Ellie
being gay like Bill and Frank, and if that’s

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:12.889
the case then ooo boy they’re just a lost
cause at that point.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:17.040
Then besides all these great positives, there
are still the couple aspects about this episode

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.360
that bothered me.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.960
The first one is Neil Druckman not intercutting
enough between the present and the past.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:26.860
To make this episode feel more productive
in the present in terms of plot, I think they

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.880
should have given Ellie more to do.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.030
That could then also allow more transition
points between the present and the past.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:36.840
Because currently, all Ellie does in the present
is search the house and finds something to

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.300
stitch up Joel with.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:42.729
If I were writing this, I probably would have
included an objective for Ellie to achieve

00:07:42.729 --> 00:07:46.450
like there being a CVS or something across
the street that she has to search.

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:51.050
And in there maybe she has to overcome a clicker
on her own which could also be a mirror between

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.370
her fighting the infected in the past.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:57.259
But this time maybe she doesn’t fight it
and instead outsmarts it by luring it away

00:07:57.259 --> 00:07:59.169
with bottles like in the game.

00:07:59.169 --> 00:08:02.940
Or even adding in another transition point
where she confronts the fact of her being

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.440
upset that she’s been left alone by Riley,
to then cutting back to her being scared Joel

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.099
will do the same and her frantically searching
the house and finding nothing.

00:08:11.099 --> 00:08:15.639
Which then she has to make a critical choice
to put herself in danger to save Joel by searching

00:08:15.639 --> 00:08:16.810
the CVS.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:20.641
Personally, I think this would alleviate that
feeling of there not being enough time spent

00:08:20.641 --> 00:08:21.840
in the present.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.940
It would give just enough progression to be
satisfying while also still being able to

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:27.760
tie into the past.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:31.319
That and if I had to choose an episode to
be over an hour long, then I’d probably

00:08:31.319 --> 00:08:32.730
choose this one.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.880
Because another issue I had was that the ending
felt abrupt.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.180
I thought we were going to get a scene where
Ellie and Riley spend the night in the mall

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:43.800
together and then the next morning we have
Ellie waking up to Riley being infected.

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.130
Essentially the mirror to episode 5.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:50.380
To me, it would elevate the tragic nature
of this whole scenario and having Ellie need

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:54.960
to kill Riley and seeing her struggle to do
so would be insanely sad.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.490
Seeing her breakdown after just killing an
infected Riley from survival would be a harrowing

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.649
scene to watch to say the least.

00:09:01.649 --> 00:09:04.070
So I’m surprised that Neil Druckman didn’t
go there.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:08.180
I haven’t played the DLC so I don’t know
if that was cut or if that wasn’t originally

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:09.180
in there.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:11.770
But I would have loved to see that explored
and there’s, even more, they could have

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:13.240
done past that.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:17.140
Like with the paranoia of Ellie potentially
turning because as they said in the episode,

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.079
it could take hours or even days.

00:09:19.079 --> 00:09:23.740
It also would have been nice to see Marlene
eventually showing up and finding Ellie in

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:24.740
this state.

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:27.970
That’s where I thought the episode would
have ended and it feels like a missed opportunity

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:30.400
overall to end it so soon.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:34.620
Just as I was ready to be rocked and floored
emotionally the episode ended and I couldn’t

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:37.290
help but feel disappointed in that moment.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.529
Regardless though, I still find the episode
very enjoyable and one other minor thing that

00:09:41.529 --> 00:09:45.920
bugged me was how dark it was in the beginning
when Riley and Ellie were on the rooftops.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.220
There was a moment where they were like, there’s
the mall, that’s it, it’s right there,

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:51.570
and I was like huhh?

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:52.570
Where?

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.329
It looked like a black abyss from how dark
it was.

00:09:54.329 --> 00:09:58.019
It’s kind of hard to be mad at this scene
though since they need to be sneaking around

00:09:58.019 --> 00:09:59.019
at night.

00:09:59.019 --> 00:10:03.080
However, it is a coincidence that I only ever
have this problem occasionally with other

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:06.700
HBO shows like Game of Thrones and House of
the Dragon.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:09.140
It might just have to do with how they color-correct
the footage.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:14.410
But yeah, overall I still liked this episode
despite it not having current plot progression.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:19.220
Where I’d rank it in comparison to the other
episodes would probably be in the lower bracket

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:23.410
and that’s mainly because of the fact of
how great they’ve all been so far.

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:25.040
So competition is fierce.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:29.149
Thank you for watching and let me know what
you thought about episode 7 of The Last of

00:10:29.149 --> 00:10:29.740
Us.

